internal/clock/pid.go:39:14 replace 0 with -1 # equivalent: dead write. p.prevErr = 0 sits in reset() (pid.go:37-41) which also sets p.primed = false (pid.go:40). The only read of prevErr in the package is pid.go:30, guarded by `if p.primed`; every execution path from reset() to that read must pass through step(), whose first invocation writes p.prevErr = err (pid.go:32) before setting primed = true (pid.go:33), so the reset value can never be observed. No production caller exists (repo grep: the only call site is internal/clock/pid_test.go:97, whose post-reset assertions step first and so also cannot observe it), but even a future caller cannot: the write is structurally dead. Full ./internal/clock suite green with the mutant hand-applied.
internal/clock/pid.go:39:14 replace 0 with 1 # equivalent: dead write, identical argument to the -1 variant. reset() (pid.go:37-41) clears primed at pid.go:40, the sole prevErr read at pid.go:30 is behind `if p.primed`, and any step() between reset and that read overwrites prevErr at pid.go:32 before priming at pid.go:33. The stored value is unobservable through every path, including pid_test.go:97's step-after-reset assertions. Full ./internal/clock suite green with the mutant hand-applied.
